WEBVTT

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:22.700
<v Ira Glass>Alex was seven. He had a Teddy bear named Tony. Tony had been with him forever. Tony was with him back when he had all sorts of surgery and nobody was sure if he was going to pull through. But Alex did pull through. And afterwards, when the nerves of the family were still jangling, Alex continued to carry around Tony for years, sleep with Tony.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯当时七岁。他有一个叫托尼的泰迪熊。托尼一直陪伴着他。早在亚历克斯经历各种手术、没人确定他能否挺过来的时候，托尼就一直陪着他。但亚历克斯挺过来了。在那之后，当家人的神经依然紧绷时，亚历克斯坚持抱着托尼很多年，和托尼一起睡觉。

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:37.090
<v Ira Glass>When he was seven, he took Tony with him to Disneyland. And, when the family got back home to San Francisco from Disneyland, Alex's parents unpacked the bags and looked for Tony. And Tony was not there. Alex's dad, Jon, tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>七岁时，他带着托尼去了迪士尼乐园。当一家人从迪士尼回到旧金山的家时，亚历克斯的父母打开行李寻找托尼，但托尼不在里面。亚历克斯的父亲乔恩讲述了这个故事。

00:00:37.090 --> 00:01:11.850
<v Jon Holtzman>So Alex was in the room and we told him it appears that Tony isn't here. And it took him about a minute to really realize that Tony was gone. And, at that point, he said two things. The first thing is he said that he couldn't imagine life without Tony, and the other is he said, "I feel like I've lost my spirit."
<v Jon Holtzman>当时亚历克斯也在房间里，我们告诉他托尼好像不在这里。他花了一分钟才真正意识到托尼不见了。那时，他说了两件事。第一件事是他说他无法想象没有托尼的生活，另一件事是他说：“我觉得我失去了我的精神支柱。”

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:30.000
<v Ira Glass>At that point, what's a parent to do? You know, what do you do? So they call the hotel, and the hotel says over the phone they have not found this Teddy bear. And so Jon flew down there the next morning. He tried to be a good dad, rented a car, retraced the family's path, made his way to the hotel.
<v Ira Glass>在那时，做父母的能怎么办呢？你知道，你能做什么？所以他们给酒店打了电话，酒店在电话里说他们没有找到这个泰迪熊。于是乔恩第二天早上飞到了那里。他努力做一个好爸爸，租了一辆车，沿着家人走过的路回溯，去了酒店。

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:51.360
<v Jon Holtzman>After waiting for a few minutes in the lobby, a security guard came and said, "Well, Mr. Holtzman, we have bad news for you. We know what happened to the bear." And they explained that a maid had seen the bear, apparently, in the garbage and that he'd been thrown away.
<v Jon Holtzman>在大厅等了几分钟后，一位保安过来对我说：“霍尔茨曼先生，我们有个坏消息。我们知道那只熊怎么了。”他们解释说，一位女佣在垃圾堆里看到了那只熊，显然它被扔掉了。

00:01:51.360 --> 00:02:18.260
<v Jon Holtzman>And so my immediate reaction was well, that's great. That's not bad news. That's good news because at least we know where he is. And he said, "Well, no. You've got to see the problem here." And we went around to the back. And there was this huge sealed dumpster with a compacter on the front, literally, the size of a semi trailer and 25, 30 feet long, at least.
<v Jon Holtzman>我当时的直接反应是：太好了。那不是坏消息。那是好消息，因为至少我们知道它在哪里。他说：“不，你得看看这里的问题。”我们绕到了后面。那里有一个巨大的密封垃圾箱，前面装有压缩机，字面上讲，它有半挂车那么大，至少有25到30英尺长。

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:24.940
<v Ira Glass>They looked at it and there was, literally, no way into this dumpster. Jon started to despair.
<v Ira Glass>他们看了看，发现真的没有办法进入这个垃圾箱。乔恩开始绝望了。

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:53.960
<v Jon Holtzman>And I started asking, "Well, when is the next garbage pickup?" And they said, "Well, it's a day from now." And I'm thinking what am I going to do? And, just as we were having this discussion, the truck arrives to pick up this giant dumpster a day early. And this was kind of an amazing thing because, if I had gotten there 15 minutes later, or 20 minutes later, the garbage truck would've been gone.
<v Jon Holtzman>我开始问：“好吧，下一次垃圾清运是什么时候？”他们说：“就在一天后。”我在想我该怎么办？就在我们进行讨论时，卡车提前一天到达，来清运这个巨大的垃圾箱。这真是一件了不起的事情，因为如果我晚到15分钟或20分钟，垃圾车就已经开走了。

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:29.020
<v Ira Glass>A squad of hotel employees was deployed to travel with Jon and the garbage truck to the recycling plant to search for the Teddy bear. And, you know, there are only two possible attitudes that adults would have towards this particular mission. One, they would be into it, or, two, they would think that Jon's a nut. And, incredibly, none of them thought that he was a nut. But why? Because almost all of them were parents. And, at some point or another, almost all of them had been in the same or a similar situation.
<v Ira Glass>一支酒店员工小队被派去随乔恩和垃圾车一起前往回收站寻找泰迪熊。你知道，成年人对这项特殊任务只有两种可能的态度。要么，他们会全身心投入，要么，他们会认为乔恩是个疯子。令人难以置信的是，他们中没有人认为他是个疯子。但为什么呢？因为他们几乎都是父母。在某个时候，他们几乎都经历过相同或类似的情况。

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:46.820
<v Jon Holtzman>I mean, you couldn't be a parent without having dealt with your kids dealing with loss, you know? And it's so horrible. And, yeah. I mean, I think everybody rallied to exactly that cause.
<v Jon Holtzman>我的意思是，作为父母，不可能没处理过孩子面对失去的情况，你知道吗？这太可怕了。是的。我想每个人都完全支持这一事业。

00:03:46.820 --> 00:04:01.580
<v Ira Glass>It's interesting because I think that a lot of parents get to points, at different points in their child's lives, where they want to intervene, and they feel like they will not allow fate to hurt their child.
<v Ira Glass>这很有趣，因为我认为许多父母在孩子生活的不同阶段都会想要干预，他们觉得他们不会允许命运伤害他们的孩子。

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:02.160
<v Jon Holtzman>Absolutely.
<v Jon Holtzman>绝对是这样。

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.470
<v Ira Glass>And they will simply do whatever they can to stop fate.
<v Ira Glass>他们会尽其所能去阻止命运。

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:27.750
<v Jon Holtzman>There's no question. And, you know, if you've ever had a child who almost dies, it means a lot, in a way, to be able to protect your kids from that kind of thing.
<v Jon Holtzman>毫无疑问。而且，如果你曾经有一个差点死去的孩子，在某种程度上，能够保护你的孩子免受那种事情的伤害意味着很多。

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:40.080
<v Ira Glass>Well, on this Father's Day, we bring you stories of dads trying to protect their kids and kids trying to protect their dads. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>在这个父亲节，我们为您带来关于父亲试图保护孩子以及孩子试图保护父亲的故事。来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:04:40.080 --> 00:05:29.190
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, the protection racket that is parenthood. Our program today in three acts. Act One, Paddington's Day at the Dump, the story of a man, a small toy bear, and tens of thousands of pounds of soggy garbage. Act Two, A Trip Down Memory Lane. A son takes his father on one, last road trip to jog his dad's failing memory of his own life. Act Three, Age of Enchantment. Writer Lawrence Weschler and his daughter, Sarah, talk about something that he did intending to be kind, intending to protect her from disappointment, that inadvertently broke her heart, and how she saved the day for the two of them. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，为人父母是一场保护者的较量。我们今天的节目分为三幕。第一幕，“帕丁顿的垃圾场之旅”，讲述了一个男人、一只玩具小熊和数万磅潮湿垃圾的故事。第二幕，“记忆小径之旅”。儿子带着父亲进行最后一次公路旅行，以唤起父亲对自己生命中那段正在衰退的记忆。第三幕，“魔幻时代”。作家劳伦斯·韦斯勒和他的女儿莎拉谈论了一些他出于好意、为了保护她免受失望而做的事情，这些事无意中伤了她的心，以及她如何为他们俩扭转了局面。请继续收听。

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:50.980
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Paddington's Day at the Dump. So Jon Holtzman and the hotel employees followed the garbage truck that's hauling the dumpster away. In his car, four conscripts. And, at this point, essentially, this story becomes the movie, Saving Private Ryan. And, you know, why don't we have some of the music from the movie just to set the right mood?
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“帕丁顿的垃圾场之旅”。乔恩·霍尔茨曼和酒店员工跟着运走垃圾箱的垃圾车。他的车里坐着四个征召来的人。在这一点上，本质上，这个故事变成了电影《拯救大兵瑞恩》。既然如此，为什么我们不放一点电影里的音乐来营造合适的氛围呢？

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:59.640
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "HYMN TO THE FALLEN" FROM SAVING PRIVATE RYAN]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 《拯救大兵瑞恩》中的《献给阵亡者的赞美诗》]

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:09.620
<v Ira Glass>Perfect. Somewhere in Southern California there's a little bear named Tony. And these four recruits are going to get him out to assuage the grief of one American family.
<v Ira Glass>太完美了。在南加州的某个地方有一只叫托尼的小熊。这四个新兵要把它救出来，以抚慰一个美国家庭的悲伤。

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:33.920
<v Jon Holtzman>OK, so we're in the car and there is this kind of silence. I made a deal with everybody in the car. I offered each of them $100 for the person who found the bear. And we were kind of plotting strategy of, well, OK, when they open this dumpster, how are we going to find the bear? Where is he going to be in the dumpster? How many days of garbage is there? And, you know.
<v Jon Holtzman>好了，我们在车里，有一种沉默。我和车里的每个人做了一笔交易。我提出给找到熊的人每人100美元。我们正在策划策略，好吧，当他们打开这个垃圾箱时，我们要怎么找到这只熊？它会在垃圾箱的哪个位置？有多少天的垃圾？你知道的。

00:06:33.920 --> 00:07:07.530
<v Jon Holtzman>And so we finally arrive at the recycling plant. And, first of all, the scope of this plant is not at all what I'd imagined. I expected it to be just a garbage dump. But, instead, it's what I imagine to be, essentially, a modern recycling plant. And there were these huge caterpillar trailers and tractors running around the plant. And there were these huge steel floors. And the scope, the scale, of the place was just really awesome.
<v Jon Holtzman>我们终于到达了回收站。首先，这个工厂的规模完全超出了我的想象。我以为它只是一个垃圾场。但相反，它是我心目中本质上的现代回收工厂。厂里有巨大的卡特彼勒拖车和拖拉机在跑来跑去。还有巨大的钢制地面。那个地方的规模和范围真是太震撼了。

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:08.780
<v Ira Glass>It's like acres is what you're trying--
<v Ira Glass>你试着说的是占地面积大得惊人——

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:15.870
<v Jon Holtzman>Yeah, it's just huge.
<v Jon Holtzman>是的，它太大了。

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:29.590
<v Ira Glass>It takes a long, long time to talk their way into the plant. If they enter, all work at the recycling center has to stop. But, incredibly, finally, they're allowed in under one condition.
<v Ira Glass>花了好长好长时间才说服他们让我们进入工厂。如果我们进去，回收中心所有的工作都必须停止。但令人难以置信的是，最后，他们同意我们在一个条件下进入。

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:50.510
<v Jon Holtzman>We would have 15 minutes to search this pile. But that's all we could have, which put a lot of pressure on us. Although, I'm not sure we understood how much pressure it would put on us until they dumped the garbage out of the trailer.
<v Jon Holtzman>我们有15分钟的时间搜查这个垃圾堆。但我们只有这些时间，这给了我们很大的压力。虽然，我不确定在他们把垃圾从拖车里倒出来之前，我们是否理解这会给我们带来多大的压力。

00:07:50.510 --> 00:08:24.300
<v Jon Holtzman>So they dumped the garbage out of the trailer. And, even though the trailer was large, I guess I'd kind of underestimated how much garbage can go in a trailer that size. And so they dumped this out on the floor. And, of course, it spread out, so it occupied this really massive space on the floor. And it was full of this brown sludge, and liquid, and just tens of thousands of plastic bags floating, essentially. Not really floating, but oozing in this--
<v Jon Holtzman>他们把垃圾从拖车里倒了出来。虽然拖车很大，但我猜我还是低估了那个尺寸的拖车能装多少垃圾。他们把垃圾倒在地上。当然，它散开了，所以它占据了地板上一个非常大的空间。里面全是棕色的污泥、液体，基本上到处都是成千上万的塑料袋。并不是真的漂浮，而是在这种……中渗出——

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.960
<v Ira Glass>This soggy sort of--
<v Ira Glass>这种潮湿的……

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:43.580
<v Jon Holtzman>--soggy mess. Exactly. And I took one look at this pile and my heart truly sank because I just thought I don't even know where to begin.
<v Jon Holtzman>——潮湿的烂摊子。完全正确。我看了一眼这个堆积物，心真的沉了下去，因为我根本不知道从哪里开始。

00:08:43.580 --> 00:09:29.310
<v Jon Holtzman>A number of the people who came with me jumped in and immediately started ripping open bags trying to find the bear. And so, after 30 seconds or so of taking in the enormity of the thing, I just plunged into this pile along with them. And, watching us do it, these garbage men, the folks who were running the recycling plant, at least four or five of them put on their gloves and started doing the same thing with us. So, at this point, there were a group of maybe eight or nine of us pulling apart bags and frantically looking for this bear.
<v Jon Holtzman>和我一起来的一些人跳进去，立即开始撕开袋子试图寻找那只熊。所以在领会了事情的艰巨性大约30秒后，我也和他们一起跳进了这堆垃圾里。看着我们这样做，那些垃圾工，那些经营回收工厂的人，至少有四五个戴上手套开始和我们做同样的事情。所以，此时此刻，我们大约有八九个人在拼命地拉开袋子，疯狂地寻找这只熊。

00:09:29.310 --> 00:10:17.530
<v Jon Holtzman>Seven or eight minutes into it I just despaired of ever finding the bear. And I couldn't believe it, you know? We'd come this far, you know? And it just seemed that it ought to work out, but I didn't see how it would. It was just too big. And I remember saying to myself, "Tony, if you're here, you're really just going to have to appear on the surface because there's just no way. I mean, the only way this is going to happen is if you make yourself appear." And the next thing I knew I was holding the bear.
<v Jon Holtzman>七八分钟过去了，我绝望了，觉得永远找不到这只熊了。我简直不敢相信，你知道吗？我们已经走了这么远，你知道吗？这似乎应该会有结果，但我看不出怎么会有。它实在太大了。我记得对自己说：“托尼，如果你在这里，你真的必须出现在表面上，因为实在没别的方法了。我的意思是，唯一能发生这种情况的方法就是你自己现身。”下一秒，我就发现我手里拿着那只熊。

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:20.550
<v Ira Glass>Wait a second. What do you mean? Do you mean you picked up a plastic bag and you shook--
<v Ira Glass>等等。你是说你捡起了一个塑料袋，然后你摇了摇——

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:33.290
<v Jon Holtzman>I picked up a plastic bag, and there he was. And he was in a plastic bag, so he was completely dry and unscathed.
<v Jon Holtzman>我捡起了一个塑料袋，它就在里面。它在一个塑料袋里，所以它完全干燥且完好无损。

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:34.870
<v Ira Glass>How do you explain that?
<v Ira Glass>你怎么解释这一点？

00:10:34.870 --> 00:11:02.380
<v Jon Holtzman>Oh, I don't. You know, I'm a rationalist. I'm not religious. I'm not really spiritual in any way. I'm a labor negotiator. I don't explain it. You know, the thing that's strange to me is not just the way I found him, but we had eight or nine people looking, you know? And why was I the one who found him?
<v Jon Holtzman>哦，我解释不了。你知道，我是个理性主义者。我不信教。我在任何方面都不太讲究精神层面。我是个劳资谈判代表。我不解释它。你知道，对我来说奇怪的不仅是我找到它的方式，还有我们有八九个人在找，你知道吗？为什么偏偏是我找到了它？

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:05.960
<v Ira Glass>Because you were touched by an angel that wanted to save you $100?
<v Ira Glass>因为你被一位想帮你省下100美元的天使触碰了？

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:15.070
<v Jon Holtzman>Yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Jon Holtzman>是的。[笑]

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:27.500
<v Ira Glass>Jon Holtzman is an attorney in San Francisco. The hotel that deployed its forces to get the bear back, I feel like we should give them credit. Let's say their name on the air. It's the Sheraton Anaheim.
<v Ira Glass>乔恩·霍尔茨曼是旧金山的一名律师。那家部署力量找回熊的酒店，我觉得我们应该给予他们赞扬。让我们在广播里说出他们的名字。是安纳海姆喜来登酒店。

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:41.060
<v Ira Glass>But one last note. Here is the thing about being a father, being a parent. Jon gets the bear. His whole squad is giddy, completely giddy, on the drive back to the hotel. And there, he calls his son to give him the news.
<v Ira Glass>但最后还有一个说明。关于作为父亲、作为父母，这就是重点。乔恩拿到了熊。在开车回酒店的路上，他的一整支小队都兴高采烈。在那里，他打电话给儿子告诉他这个消息。

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:50.060
<v Jon Holtzman>He was really, really happy. But there was also a slightly of-course-ish response to the thing, which is, "Well, good. I'm glad you got the bear."
<v Jon Holtzman>他真的、真的很高兴。但也有一些理所当然的反应，那就是：“哦，好的。我很高兴你找回了那只熊。”

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:53.280
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't quite understand it for the heroic gesture that it is.
<v Ira Glass>他没有完全理解这是多么英雄的行为。

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:54.820
<v Jon Holtzman>I think that's true.
<v Jon Holtzman>我想那是真的。

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:57.350
<v Ira Glass>He's like, oh, yeah. Dad went down and got the bear.
<v Ira Glass>他就像是，哦，是的。爸爸下去把熊拿回来了。

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:58.580
<v Jon Holtzman>That's completely true.
<v Jon Holtzman>那是完全正确的。

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:01.650
<v Ira Glass>The theme to Star Wars is not playing in his head as that happens.
<v Ira Glass>当这件事发生时，《星球大战》的主题曲并没有在他脑海中响起。

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:15.740
<v Jon Holtzman>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jon Holtzman>[笑声]

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:31.250
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Trip Down Memory Lane. Joel Meyerowitz is a photographer. He grew up in New York City. In the 1970's, his parents moved to Florida. Joel would visit for a few days at a time. As time passed, his father, Hy, developed Alzheimer's.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“记忆小径之旅”。乔尔·迈耶罗维茨是一位摄影师。他在纽约市长大。20世纪70年代，他的父母搬到了佛罗里达。乔尔每次会去探望几天。随着时间的推移，他的父亲海伊患上了阿尔茨海默症。

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:45.660
<v Ira Glass>Before he retired, Hy was a salesman for 40 years. He did a few years as a comic in a vaudeville act before that. He was a boxer. He won his weight class in the first Golden Gloves competition. But now that he had Alzheimer's, the doctor said that he should stay inside, avoid a lot of stimulation.
<v Ira Glass>在退休前，海伊做了40年的销售员。在此之前，他还在杂耍表演中做了几年的喜剧演员。他是一名拳击手。他在第一届金手套比赛中赢得了他的体重级别。但现在他得了阿尔茨海默症，医生说他应该待在室内，避免过多的刺激。

00:12:45.660 --> 00:13:12.540
<v Ira Glass>His son, Joel, thought, what if we tried, for a brief time, something different? He wanted to have one last adventure with his father, one last road trip before the Alzheimer's made it impossible to have more adventures. Joel also hoped that, maybe, being on the road, visiting places that he'd been, going back to the old neighborhood one more time, might spark some memories that had been gone for his dad, that had been long, long vanished. It was a gift to his dad.
<v Ira Glass>他的儿子乔尔想，如果我们尝试在短时间内做一些不同的事情会怎样？他想在阿尔茨海默症让他无法再进行冒险之前，和父亲进行最后一次冒险，最后一次公路旅行。乔尔还希望，也许在路上，访问他曾经去过的地方，再回到老街区一次，可能会激发他父亲早已消失、早已消逝的记忆。这是给他父亲的一份礼物。

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:34.160
<v Ira Glass>So they got a camera. They went on a three-week trip driving from Fort Lauderdale back to New York City. Joel Meyerowitz's son, Sasha, filmed lots of the trip. You actually hear his voice only a few times in this footage I'm about to play you. When they set out on the trip, Sasha was 27, Joel was 57, Hy was 87.
<v Ira Glass>于是他们拿了一台摄像机。他们进行了为期三周的旅行，从劳德代尔堡开车回纽约市。乔尔·迈耶罗维茨的儿子萨沙拍摄了很多旅行的片段。你实际上只在我要播放的这段录像中几次听到他的声音。当他们踏上旅程时，萨沙27岁，乔尔57岁，海伊87岁。

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:37.500
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Do you know what ocean this is?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你知道这是什么大洋吗？

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:44.410
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Well, I'll be honest with you, Joel, I don't know remember. Not the Catskills ocean?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯，我老实告诉你，乔尔，我不记得了。不是卡茨基尔大洋吧？

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:45.310
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Catskills are mountains.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>卡茨基尔是山脉。

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:46.070
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:47.240
<v Joel Meyerowitz>This is an ocean.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>这是一片大洋。

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:48.220
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Uh-huh.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯哼。

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:48.750
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Which one?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>是哪一个？

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.480
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Well, I don't know.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯，我不知道。

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.940
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Is it the Pacific or the Atlantic?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>是太平洋还是大西洋？

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:54.860
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I would take it as the Pacific.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>我会认为是太平洋。

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:55.350
<v Joel Meyerowitz>[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Joel Meyerowitz>[听不清]

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:56.810
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Wrong.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>错了。

00:13:56.810 --> 00:13:59.240
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[LAUGHTER]
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[笑声]

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:00.720
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah, wrong.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的，错了。

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:02.200
<v Joel Meyerowitz>So, which one is it then?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>那到底是哪一个？

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.180
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Either the Permissia? or the--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>要么是佩密西？还是——

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:05.510
<v Joel Meyerowitz>The Permissic. Right.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>佩密西。对。

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:05.890
<v Hy Meyerowitz>The Permissic?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>佩密西？

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:08.160
<v Joel Meyerowitz>The Pershimmoth. The Pershimmoth ocean.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>波什摩斯。波什摩斯大洋。

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:20.510
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I am at the Pershimmoth. What are you asking me so early in the morning a question like that?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>我在波什摩斯。一大早你为什么要问我这种问题？

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:22.540
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Where were you born?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你出生在哪里？

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:36.810
<v Hy Meyerowitz>1092-- 109207-- no. 159059 East--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>1092—— 109207—— 不。东159059——

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.510
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Well, it was East 100th Street, right? Yeah?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>嗯，是在东100街，对吧？是吗？

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:51.440
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。是的。

00:14:51.440 --> 00:15:12.090
<v Joel Meyerowitz>He taught me all the street smart things. He set up a punching bag for me. And he taught me how to fight, how to take my stance, how to throw a punch, how to put your body behind it. He had me work out on a light bag too. Like this, you know?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>他教了我所有街头的生存之道。他为我架了一个沙袋。他教我如何打架，如何站位，如何出拳，如何把身体的力量集中在拳头上。他还让我练习轻沙袋。像这样，你知道吗？

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:33.250
<v Joel Meyerowitz>He always felt that, if you got into a scrape in a neighborhood, that you shouldn't run away. His motto was, "Step in and deliver the first blow." And he said, "Take the biggest guy down." And that was his attitude. He wouldn't stop and run away from anything.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>他总是觉得，如果你在街区里陷入争斗，你不应该逃跑。他的座右铭是，“冲进去，打出第一拳。”他说，“把块头最大的家伙放倒。”这就是他的态度。他不会停下来逃避任何事情。

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:52.530
<v Joel Meyerowitz>On Morrison Avenue, the block we lived on, he was considered the mayor of the block. Any time there was a dispute that had to be settled, they would actually come and ask him, "What do you think, Hy?" Or, if there were strangers coming through the neighborhood and they needed to be told to get out of there, they'd get him.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>在莫里森大道，我们住的那条街上，他被认为是该街区的市长。任何时候有争执需要解决，人们真的会来问他，“你觉得呢，海伊？”或者，如果有陌生人穿过街区，需要有人告诉他们离开那里，他们就会找他。

00:15:52.530 --> 00:16:12.970
<v Joel Meyerowitz>The scenes I remember are that people would gather in front of our window and they'd yell, "Hymie. Hey, Hymie." And he'd come to the window. And he would adjudicate from the window. He would say, "No. No, you shouldn't do that." And, "He should do this. And, "You get that. Let him alone." It was like the judge.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我记得的场景是人们聚集在我们的窗前大喊：“海米。嘿，海米。”他会来到窗前。他会在窗口进行裁决。他说：“不。不，你不应该那样做。”还有，“他应该这样做。”以及，“你拿到那个。让他一个人待着。”他就像那个法官。

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:17.850
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Here they come, baby. Yeah, watch him now. Hey, ya.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>他们来了，宝贝。是的，看着他现在。嘿，呀。

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:19.230
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[BIRD SCREECH]
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[鸟鸣]

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:27.040
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Hello. Hello. Hello. Hello.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>你好。你好。你好。你好。

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:28.400
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Hey, Pop.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>嘿，老爸。

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:28.850
<v Hy Meyerowitz>What?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>什么？

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:32.040
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Do you know what this reminds me of?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你知道这让我想起什么吗？

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:32.750
<v Hy Meyerowitz>No.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>不知道。

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.450
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Does that remind you of Melvin?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>这让你想起梅尔文了吗？

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:36.690
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Oh, yeah. Yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>哦，是的。是的。

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:37.190
<v Joel Meyerowitz>That's what I was thinking.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我也是这么想的。

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.670
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah. You didn't think of the neighbor that time.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的，你上次没想起那个邻居。

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.460
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Do you remember Melvin?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你还记得梅尔文吗？

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:43.050
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah, sure, I remember him.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的，当然，我记得他。

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:44.210
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What was Melvin?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>梅尔文是什么？

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:48.070
<v Hy Meyerowitz>It was a little Chinese-- a little bird.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>那是一只小中国——一只小鸟。

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:48.690
<v Joel Meyerowitz>A parakeet.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>长尾小鹦鹉。

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:50.450
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Parakeet, right. I didn't think of the--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>长尾小鹦鹉，对。我没想起——

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:51.870
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Do you remember the things that Melvin said?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你还记得梅尔文说的话吗？

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:56.990
<v Hy Meyerowitz>He called to us. He spoke to us, and he also called his name out a lot.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>他叫我们。他跟我们说话，他也经常叫自己的名字。

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:58.290
<v Joel Meyerowitz>How did he say it? Do you remember?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>他是怎么说的？你记得吗？

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:03.070
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Hi, I'm Melvin. Hi, Melvin. Isn't it, "Hi Melvin?"
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗨，我是梅尔文。嗨，梅尔文。难道不是，“嗨梅尔文？”

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:05.920
<v Joel Meyerowitz>No. He said I'm Melvin, what?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>不。他说我是梅尔文，什么？

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:07.170
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Melvin Belvin?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>梅尔文·贝尔文？

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:08.480
<v Joel Meyerowitz>I'm Melvin Meyerowitz.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我是梅尔文·迈耶罗维茨。

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.310
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Oh, yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Hy Meyerowitz>哦，是的。[笑]

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:12.580
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Can you tell me the story of the time he--
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你能告诉我他那次的故事吗——

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:25.780
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I can't tell you about it. Look at this. I can't tell you about it. And look at this. Another one coming. Hi, boys. Hello. Hello. We know you. We know your grandfather.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>我没法告诉你那个。看看这个。我没法告诉你那个。再看看这个。又来了一只。嗨，伙计们。你好。你好。我们认识你。我们认识你爷爷。

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:30.890
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Pop, remember how he used to say my name is Melvin Meyerowitz, I'm a Jewish bird?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>老爸，还记得他以前是怎么说我的名字是梅尔文·迈耶罗维茨，我是一只犹太鸟吗？

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:32.070
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yes. Yes.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。是的。

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.390
<v Joel Meyerowitz>That comes back to you?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>想起来了吗？

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:35.930
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I know it. It didn't. But that--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>我知道。没想起来。但这——

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:37.560
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Good morning, Doctor Goldberg?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>早上好，戈德堡医生？

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:38.380
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Oh, yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>哦，是的。

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.950
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Remember Dr. Goldberg came to visit Mom when she was sick?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>记得戈德堡医生在妈妈生病时来看她吗？

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:41.780
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:52.500
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Do you remember the time the bird flew away across the Bronx? He went out the door on Mom's shoulder and he flew away? And we were all so heartbroken that the bird was gone.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你记得那次那只鸟飞过布朗克斯吗？它从妈妈肩膀上飞出门外跑掉了？我们当时都心碎了，因为鸟不见了。

00:17:52.500 --> 00:18:21.840
<v Joel Meyerowitz>And then some woman called up. Do you remember? She called Mom and she said, "Do you have a bird named Melvin?" My mother said, "Yeah, we have a bird. It was a green and yellow parakeet." And the woman said, "Well, the bird landed on my window and said, 'My name is Melvin Meyerowitz. I'm a Jewish bird.' And I looked you up in the phone book. And I called a Meyerowitz, and they said, 'Oh, no, that's my brother Hy's bird." We went and we got the bird back. Do you remember that?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>后来有个女人打电话来。你记得吗？她打电话给妈妈说，“你们有一只叫梅尔文的鸟吗？”我妈妈说，“是的，我们有一只鸟。那是一只绿色和黄色的小鹦鹉。”那个女人说，“好吧，这只鸟落在我的窗台上说，‘我的名字是梅尔文·迈耶罗维茨。我是一只犹太鸟。’我在电话簿里查到了你们。我打给一个迈耶罗维茨，他们说，‘哦，不，那是我的兄弟海伊的鸟。”我们就去把鸟接回来了。你记得吗？

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.480
<v Hy Meyerowitz>No.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>不。

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:29.060
<v Joel Meyerowitz>I'm surprised.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我很惊讶。

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:33.680
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Come on. You want to go? You want to go-- don't fall. Take it easy.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>来吧。你想走吗？你想去——别摔倒。慢慢来。

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:33.940
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[BIRD SCREECH]
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[鸟鸣]

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:35.460
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Take it easy.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>慢慢来。

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:35.820
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[BIRD SCREECH]
<v Hy Meyerowitz>[鸟鸣]

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:47.360
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Do you want to go?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>你想走吗？

00:18:47.360 --> 00:19:12.710
<v Joel Meyerowitz>With the onset of memory loss, it's not only his memories that are fading away, but it's the memories that I shared with him that are fading away. So I could no longer say to him, "Hey, Pop. Remember, we did this," and have him say, "Yes, that was fantastic," remember it. So I found myself progressively left alone with my memories.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>随着记忆丧失的开始，不仅仅是他的记忆正在消失，我与他分享的记忆也在消失。所以我不能再对他说，“嘿，老爸。记得我们做过这个吗？”然后让他说，“是的，那太棒了，”并记得它。所以我发现自己逐渐独自面对我的记忆。

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:52.690
<v Joel Meyerowitz>And then you look at your own memories and you realize, I've got this handful of really insignificant things, and I've made them my world, my world of memory. And it's astonishing how the few things that I recall to share with him are minor notes, you know, the bird or the handful of things that I ask him. So, that was a lesson, for me, about what it is that rises up out of our experience that we hold onto.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>然后你看看你自己的记忆，你会意识到，我手里只有这一把非常微不足道的事情，我把它们变成了我的世界，我的记忆世界。令人惊讶的是，我回忆起与他分享的那些事情都是些小插曲，你知道，鸟或者是那些我问他的事情。所以，那对我来说是一堂课，关于我们从经历中升华出来并坚持保留的东西。

00:19:52.690 --> 00:19:53.630
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Pop?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>老爸？

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:54.480
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yes.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:56.550
<v Joel Meyerowitz>I want to ask you a few questions about the family.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我想问你几个关于家庭的问题。

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:57.890
<v Hy Meyerowitz>About our family?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>关于我们家庭？

00:19:57.890 --> 00:19:58.730
<v Joel Meyerowitz>About our family.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>关于我们家庭。

00:19:58.730 --> 00:19:59.990
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yes.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:01.370
<v Joel Meyerowitz>You've got three sons.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你有三个儿子。

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:02.180
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yes.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:05.210
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What are their names?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>他们叫什么名字？

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:10.590
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Joel, Ricky--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>乔尔，里奇——

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.080
<v Joel Meyerowitz>And who's your youngest son?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你最小的儿子是谁？

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:22.950
<v Hy Meyerowitz>The youngest one is-- I think it's David but I don't remember his name any more.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>最小的是——我想是戴维，但我记不起他的名字了。

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:25.510
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Joel, Rick, and--
<v Joel Meyerowitz>乔尔，里克，和——

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:30.530
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Joel, Ricky, and-- yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>乔尔，里奇，和——是的。

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:31.680
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Stevie.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>史蒂夫。

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.460
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Stevie, oh, yeah.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>史蒂夫，哦，是的。

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:37.690
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Do you know what Rick does to make a living, what his career is?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你知道里克靠什么谋生，他的职业是什么吗？

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:48.780
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Well, he tries to do a lot of stuff. If he were following me, he'd be doing it on the crook, but--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯，他想做很多事。如果他跟着我，他会靠歪门邪道做，但——

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.550
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What's he famous for?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>他因什么出名？

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:51.320
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Ricky?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>里奇？

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:52.620
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Yeah.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>是的。

00:20:52.620 --> 00:21:02.480
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Well, in the first place, he's my kid. You are also. And because that alone will get you enough fame.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯，首先，他是我的孩子。你也是。仅仅这一点就足以让你出名了。

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:05.000
<v Joel Meyerowitz>So you don't know what Rick does?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>所以你不知道里克做什么？

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:14.550
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Ricky? Rick is a half-time, a part-time-- that book, I don't know the trade name.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>里奇？里克是个半职，兼职——那本书，我不知道商品名称。

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:19.060
<v Joel Meyerowitz>OK, you're getting closer, Pop. Just try. Try to think about what Rick does.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>好的，你离答案越来越近了，老爸。试一试。试着想想里克做什么。

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.660
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Rick? Rick is an artist.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>里克？里克是个艺术家。

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:23.390
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Right. You got it.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>对。你答对了。

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:24.540
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Not?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>没答对？

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:30.780
<v Joel Meyerowitz>You got it. You got it right. And I'm Joel. What do I do?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你答对了。你答对了。我是乔尔。我做什么？

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:34.400
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Ah, you're a doll. You're my best number one for--
<v Hy Meyerowitz>啊，你是个玩偶。你是我的第一号——

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:43.050
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Come on, get serious, Pop. Do you remember what it is I do? What do I do?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>别闹了，老爸。你记得我做什么吗？我做什么？

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:49.870
<v Hy Meyerowitz>You make money. [LAUGHS] I'll tell you what. I'll give it to you straight off the street.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>你赚钱。[笑] 我告诉你。我直接告诉你。

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:52.240
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What's my profession?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我的职业是什么？

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:53.160
<v Hy Meyerowitz>What's, what?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>什么，什么？

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:54.700
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What's my profession?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我的职业是什么？

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:55.970
<v Hy Meyerowitz>What's your most attachment?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>你最执着的是什么？

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:57.420
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What's my profession?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我的职业是什么？

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:08.960
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Your profession? Now I've got it figured out. I don't know, a crook, or a thief, or whatever. You're known as an artist, serious artist, in the art field.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>你的职业？现在我想通了。我不知道，一个骗子，或者小偷，或者其他什么。你在艺术领域被称为艺术家，严肃的艺术家。

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:12.650
<v Joel Meyerowitz>OK. What about Stevie? What's Stevie's business?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>好的。史蒂夫呢？史蒂夫的业务是什么？

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:13.600
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Judy?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>朱迪？

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:14.390
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Stevie.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>史蒂夫。

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:23.470
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Stevie? I don't know.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>史蒂夫？我不知道。

00:22:23.470 --> 00:22:28.750
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Does it bother you that you can't remember your kid's names sometimes? And you can't remember what they do?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>有时记不住孩子的名字会让你烦恼吗？而且你记不住他们做什么？

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:48.570
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Well, I tell you, yeah. I see them. Hello. Goodbye. And that's it. I'm not complaining.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯，我告诉你，是的。我看见他们。你好。再见。就这样。我不抱怨。

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:56.040
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>Is it hard to see him like this? Does it feel painful?
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>看到他这样很难受吗？感觉痛苦吗？

00:22:56.040 --> 00:23:31.190
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Honestly, It's sad. I mean, I have a feeling of sadness. But I also have a feeling of acceptance. We've been apart for 20 years in the mutual prime of our lives. When I was raising my children and he was a grandparent, we weren't together. And so, I guess, I'm just accepting of where he is.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>老实说，很悲伤。我的意思是，我有一种悲伤的感觉。但我也有一种接受的感觉。我们在我们人生的黄金时期分开了20年。当我抚养我的孩子时，他是祖父母，我们没有在一起。所以，我想，我只是接受他现在的处境。

00:23:31.190 --> 00:24:06.910
<v Joel Meyerowitz>If we had been together for 20 years, and I had seen the decline, and I had been relating to him emotionally and lovingly all that time, I might feel a deeper sadness. But, even though he's my father, the distance that we've been apart all these years has put some kind of a buffer in there. So this is the guy that I know now. We have a much closer relationship in the way we see each other and talk to each other and have continuity.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>如果我们在一起生活了20年，我看到了他的衰退，并且一直与他进行情感和爱的交流，我可能会感到更深层的悲伤。但即使他是我的父亲，这些年来我们分开的距离在中间设置了一种缓冲。所以这就是我现在认识的这个家伙。我们在彼此看待、交谈和保持联系的方式上有着更亲密的关系。

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:14.610
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>I just know, at one point, you said to him he was your hero. Actually, I thought it was so sweet. And I thought it must be hard to see--
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>我只是知道，在某个时候，你对他说过他是你的英雄。其实，我觉得那太贴心了。我以为看到——

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:16.300
<v Joel Meyerowitz>--your hero fall.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>——你的英雄陨落一定很难受。

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:17.180
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>Yeah, right.
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>是的，没错。

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:52.130
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Well, he was my hero, my childhood hero, you know? But that's so far away. I can't relate to the sadness of that. I mean, I love him in that unequivocal way that a child loves a parent. And I feel, when I care for him, a kind of, I guess, a renewal or a rebirth of feeling in this period.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>嗯，他是我的英雄，我童年的英雄，你知道吗？但那是太遥远的事情了。我无法将那种悲伤与现在联系起来。我的意思是，我以孩子爱父母的那种唯一方式爱着他。当我照顾他时，我感到一种，我猜，在这个时期感觉的更新或重生。

00:24:52.130 --> 00:25:28.200
<v Joel Meyerowitz>I know, when I take him after a shower and I rub him down and I actually feel his head in my hand and I feel his flesh in my hands, it's been many years since I had that kind of contact with my father. And it was a little strange at first. I thought, what's it like to rub this other person's body, touch this other person's body? There's a slight deference. And then I realize it's my pop, you know? And he's in need. He can't take care of himself this way.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我知道，当我带他洗完澡帮他擦干时，我真的用手感受他的头，我的手中感受他的肌肤，我有很多年没有和父亲有那种接触了。起初有点奇怪。我想，抚摸这个人的身体，触碰这个人的身体是什么感觉？有一点敬意。然后我意识到这是我的老爸，你知道吗？他需要照顾。他无法以这种方式照顾自己。

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:39.020
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Mom? OK, here's Papa, Mom. Hold on. OK. Here comes Dad.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>妈妈？好的，这是爸爸，妈妈。等等。好的。爸爸来了。

00:25:39.020 --> 00:26:00.580
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Joel? Is it my Joel? Don't you know who I am? Who? What do you mean how come I'm on the phone? Don't get mad at me. Don't get mad at me. It's very important because I've been traveling.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>乔尔？是我的乔尔吗？你不知道我是谁吗？谁？你什么意思问我怎么在电话上？别对我生气。别对我生气。这很重要，因为我一直在旅行。

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:28.430
<v Joel Meyerowitz>I think my father had a classic marriage of his generation. He loved my mother. She was beautiful and hot-tempered, an exciting person to be with. But he wanted something from her that either he didn't know how to get or she couldn't give. And that was a kind of mother love that he, himself, hadn't experienced.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我觉得我父亲拥有一段他那一代人的经典婚姻。他爱我母亲。她美丽且脾气暴躁，是一个令人兴奋的伴侣。但他想从她那里得到一些他要么不知道如何得到要么她给不了的东西。那是一种他自己没有经历过的母爱。

00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:44.380
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Oh, yes. I'm OK. No, I'm not having a good time. Are you having a good time? You are, really? OK. I want you to be healthy and strong and smile, Sally.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>哦，是的。我很好。不，我过得并不开心。你过得开心吗？你真的吗？好的。我希望你健康强壮，微笑，莎莉。

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:59.540
<v Joel Meyerowitz>You know, his mother had been bedridden after his birth. And then she died soon after, so he never really knew her. And that's the deepest groove in his memory, which is, I think, unrequited love.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你知道，他母亲在他出生后就卧床不起了。不久后就去世了，所以他从未真正认识她。这是他记忆中最深的沟壑，也就是，我想，未得到的回报的爱。

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:28.030
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Don't get down to judge this because, right now, I'm beginning to face one of Joel's tests. And I will be back home, maybe, who knows, a couple of months from now. I don't know, Sally. Would you go with me the next trip? Would you go with me the next trip? OK. All right, babe. Take care, sweetheart. Be careful, will you? Be careful. Bye-bye.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>别在这里做评判，因为现在，我开始面对乔尔的考验之一。我会回家的，也许，谁知道呢，几个月后。我不知道，莎莉。下次旅行你会和我一起去吗？下次旅行你会和我一起去吗？好的。好吧，宝贝。保重，亲爱的。小心点，好吗？小心点。再见。

00:27:28.030 --> 00:28:04.180
<v Joel Meyerowitz>And I think that, from my point of view now, that, if he had really just loved my mother without demanding something from her or needing something from her, which was that childhood need, that she probably would have just loved him back for the kind, warm, funny man that he was. But, because there was something that he was demanding, she couldn't give it to him. A kind of perverse logic of relationship occurred.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我觉得从我现在的观点来看，如果他当初只是爱我母亲，而不向她索取什么，或者不需要她什么，那是一种童年的需求，那么她很可能会因为他是个善良、温暖、风趣的男人而爱他。但因为他索取了一些她给不了的东西，一种关系的变态逻辑出现了。

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:08.890
<v Hy Meyerowitz>She's mad. She's mad about something. She's mad about me. I didn't do nothing.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>她生气了。她因为某事生气。她因为我生气。我什么都没做。

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.160
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Why is she mad?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>她为什么生气？

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:15.870
<v Hy Meyerowitz>She's mad that's all. She sounded very, very bad. She sounded like she didn't care.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>她生气了，就这么简单。她听起来非常、非常糟糕。她听起来好像她不在乎。

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:18.010
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Well, she probably was caring a lot.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>嗯，她可能在乎很多。

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:18.290
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Oh, sure.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>哦，当然。

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:20.680
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Which is why she was mad that you didn't call.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>这就是为什么她因为你没打电话而生气。

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:28.640
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I waver also, when I think of her being by herself. And I would rather be there than be here.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>当我想到她独自一人时，我也很动摇。我宁愿在那里也不愿在这里。

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:31.130
<v Joel Meyerowitz>But you're having such a good time here.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>但你在这里玩得很开心。

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:51.690
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah. I'm, to my limits, I'm having what I like. And I would love to be involved with [INAUDIBLE]. But the idea is that I always have to include Mama.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的。我，在我的极限内，我做着我喜欢的事情。我很想参与其中[听不清]。但想法是我总是必须带上妈妈。

00:28:51.690 --> 00:29:01.660
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Throughout the trip he asked for Sally every single day. "Where's Sally?" "Where's Mom," thinking that she should have been in the car next to him.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>在整个旅程中，他每天都问莎莉在哪里。“莎莉在哪里？”“妈妈在哪里，”认为她应该在他旁边的车里。

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:09.350
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Sally. Did you leave Sally upstairs there?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>莎莉。你把莎莉留在楼上了吗？

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:11.880
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Yeah, Pop. We said goodbye to Mom.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>是的，老爸。我们跟妈妈说再见了。

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:19.880
<v Hy Meyerowitz>No kidding? Right? Because I noticed, right here, that there's no Sally. I thought this was Sally all bundled up, and I see it's not bundled up.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>别开玩笑了？对吧？因为我注意到，就在这里，没有莎莉。我还以为这是包得严严实实的莎莉，结果发现没包起来。

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:24.770
<v Joel Meyerowitz>It's your pillow. That bed pillow's going to have to be your Sally for the next two weeks, though.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>那是你的枕头。不过那个床枕头得在接下来的两周里当你的莎莉了。

00:29:24.770 --> 00:29:42.220
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah, yeah, the pillow. Oh, you guys are starting to pull tricks on me now. I know. I know. I know. Well, is she--was she here?
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的，是的，那个枕头。哦，你们现在开始跟我耍花招了。我知道。我知道。我知道。好吧，她——她在这里吗？

00:29:42.220 --> 00:29:47.240
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Nope. That's your pillow, Pop.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>不。那是你的枕头，老爸。

00:29:47.240 --> 00:30:17.900
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Jesus Christ, almighty. I know she's not in the trunk. You wouldn't do that to her. I'm not even going to come through this Joel. You know, I'm not the smartest or the cutest in the service. If my mommy don't come up, and then don't come down in five minutes, I want to know where she is. This is my wife.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>上帝啊，万能的。我知道她不在后备箱里。你不会那样对她的。我甚至不想说这个，乔尔。你知道，我不是服务中最聪明或最可爱的。如果我的妈咪没上来，五分钟后没下来，我想知道她在哪里。这是我的妻子。

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:19.640
<v Joel Meyerowitz>So we'd better check the trunk.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>所以我们最好检查一下后备箱。

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:26.370
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Yeah, or the bed is in the trunk. I wouldn't be surprised.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>是的，或者床在后备箱里。我不会感到惊讶的。

00:30:26.370 --> 00:31:05.170
<v Joel Meyerowitz>There was a time, when we were driving with her in Florida before we made the film, and she was sitting right next to him. And he leaned over to me and he said, "Where's Mom?" And I said, "Well, who's sitting next to you?" And he looked over at this person sitting next to him and he said, "Where's Sally? That's not Sally."
<v Joel Meyerowitz>有一次，在我们拍电影前和她在佛罗里达开车时，她就坐在他旁边。他向我倾过身来说，“妈妈在哪里？”我说，“坐在你旁边的是谁？”他看着坐在他旁边的那个人说，“莎莉在哪里？那不是莎莉。”

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:29.540
<v Joel Meyerowitz>When my father was young, he was a wonderful dancer and athlete and a natural comic. And I guess Charlie Chaplin was the rage. And, just as there were Elvis imitators, there were Chaplin imitators. And, my father, he became a Chaplin imitator. And he had that act that, I guess, he took on the road or around the vaudeville circuit in New York.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>当我父亲年轻的时候，他是一位出色的舞者、运动员和天生的喜剧演员。我猜当时查理·卓别林风靡一时。就像有猫王模仿者一样，也有卓别林模仿者。我父亲，他成了卓别林模仿者。他有那个表演，我想，他带到纽约的杂耍表演巡回演出中。

00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:51.870
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I was trying to fit in myself too, all the years, with the joking. I was trying to be a Chaplin. I didn't know if I was doing that right or wrong. But I saw that poor little guy that he'd bend down, pick something up, everybody'd give him a kick in the ass. That was what I used to see. That is what I didn't want to happen to me.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>这些年来，我也试图用那些笑话融入其中。我试图成为一个卓别林。我不知道我做得是对还是错。但我看到那个可怜的小家伙弯下腰，捡起什么东西，每个人都会踢他的屁股。这就是我过去看到的。那是我不想发生在我身上的事。

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:57.640
<v Joel Meyerowitz>What was it that you liked about Charlie Chaplin, that made you want to do the Charlie Chaplin act?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>关于查理·卓别林，是什么让你想做查理·卓别林的表演？

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:13.090
<v Hy Meyerowitz>He was a giant in the height of a little midget. He was a little guy righting all the wrongs, helping others. As he'd pass by, we'd pat him on the top of the head. He was remembered out there.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>他是一个矮人身高里的巨人。他是一个扶正一切错误、帮助他人的小个子。当他经过时，我们会拍拍他的头顶。人们记得他。

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:47.290
<v Hy Meyerowitz>And I just meditate, look at the little party. And the little person had no money or no nothing. And he was going-- on the inside. He'd come out here, like this, like shake his shoulders and then traveled on. And that was what I loved, the goodness, the goodness. And not everybody understood him. But those that understood him, they would put their arm around him, and he would do the same. But it was a beautiful deed for the day.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>我只是冥想，看着那个小派对。那个小人物没钱，什么都没有。他正在——在内心深处。他会像这样出来，耸耸肩，然后继续旅行。那就是我所爱的，善良，善良。并不是每个人都理解他。但那些理解他的人，他们会把手搭在他的肩上，他也会这样做。但这对他那一天来说是一种美丽的善举。

00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:49.890
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Did you feel connected to him in that way?
<v Joel Meyerowitz>你觉得你以那种方式和他有联系吗？

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:52.530
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Always. Always. Always.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>总是。总是。总是。

00:32:52.530 --> 00:33:01.130
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Aw.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>噢。

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:48.830
<v Ira Glass>With Alzeimer's disease, most memory finally dissolves. And, even though he was a man who was easily lovable, I think he forgot that these were his qualities. And that he was, in fact, loved by people faded from his memory. And, at the end of his life, he remembers a few of the more painful things-- that he's a motherless child, that he was not loved the way he wanted to be loved. And it's amazing that, with the murkiness of Alzheimer's clouding everything, that something as primal as being an unloved child stayed with him.
<v Ira Glass>随着阿尔茨海默症的发展，大多数记忆最终都会消散。尽管他是一个很容易被爱的人，但我认为他忘记了这些都是他的品质。他事实上被人们爱过的事实从他的记忆中消失了。而在他生命的最后，他记得一些更痛苦的事情——他是一个没妈的孩子，他没有得到他想要的爱。令人惊讶的是，尽管阿尔茨海默症的阴霾笼罩着一切，但像做一个不可爱的孩子这样原始的东西却留在了他心中。

00:33:48.830 --> 00:34:11.170
<v Hy Meyerowitz>Well, everybody would show a little bit of something to me that I was accepted. And they would talk to me. And they'd pat me on the top of the head or they'd put their hands around me. I'm home.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>嗯，每个人都会对我表现出一丁点我也被接受的感觉。他们会跟我说话。他们会拍拍我的头顶，或者把手放在我身上。我回家了。

00:34:11.170 --> 00:34:37.620
<v Hy Meyerowitz>I loved everybody. I loved everybody, but nobody saw me. Nobody remembered me. Nobody knew me and nobody saw me, but I was there. And, to this very day, I have the same thing. I have poor Mommy and she doesn't see me. She doesn't see me.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>我爱每个人。我爱每个人，但没有人看到我。没有人记得我。没有人认识我，也没有人看到我，但我在这里。直到今天，我也还有同样的事情。我有可怜的妈咪，她看不到我。她看不到我。

00:34:37.620 --> 00:35:15.020
<v Joel Meyerowitz>Aw, Pop. Well, your boys saw you. We all saw you. I loved when you were the strong man in the neighborhood, but I loved when you were the Chaplin figure and the comic. I loved the way you drove the car. You were such a great driver.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>噢，老爸。嗯，你的儿子看见了你。我们都看见了。我喜欢你做街区强人的时候，但我更喜欢你做卓别林人物和喜剧演员的时候。我喜欢你开车的方式。你是一位伟大的司机。

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:45.180
<v Joel Meyerowitz>I loved the way you talked to people. You could talk to the big guys or the little guys, and you made them all the same, Pop. I used to think of you as the great equalizer. You could take a guy who was a doctor, or a principal of the school, or a business man, and you can take another guy, who was just an ordinary worker, and you would treat them the same. And you would bring them all to the same level, and that would be the level of laughter.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>我喜欢你与人交谈的方式。你能和大人物或小人物交谈，你让他们都一样，老爸。我过去常把你当作伟大的平衡者。你能带一个医生、校长或商人，也能带另一个只是普通工人的人，你会同等地对待他们。你会把他们都带到同一个水平，那就是欢笑的水平。

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:53.910
<v Hy Meyerowitz>You saw me. You saw Papa.
<v Hy Meyerowitz>你看见了我。你看见了爸爸。

00:35:53.910 --> 00:36:04.490
<v Joel Meyerowitz>That's right.
<v Joel Meyerowitz>那是对的。

00:36:04.490 --> 00:37:35.590
<v Ira Glass>Joel Meyerowitz; his father, Hy; his son, Sasha. In the year since they recorded that footage, Hy has died. Joel and Sasha cut their footage into a film. It's called, Pop. It aired on the PBS show, Frontline. This September, Joel is putting out a book, called Aftermath , with his large-format photos from the Ground Zero site in New York City. Coming up, can a dad try too hard? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>乔尔·迈耶罗维茨；他的父亲海伊；他的儿子萨沙。在他们录制那些素材后的几年里，海伊去世了。乔尔和萨沙将他们的素材剪辑成了一部电影。它叫《老爸》。它在PBS的节目《前线》中播出。今年九月，乔尔将出版一本书，名为《余波》，其中收录了他从纽约市归零地拍摄的大画幅照片。接下来，父亲会不会用力过猛？一会儿从芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台的节目中揭晓。

