<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of writers and performers to tackle that theme. Today's program, for Father's Day, we bring you stories of kids trying to care for and protect their dads and dads trying to protect and care for their kids.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，邀请各种作家和表演者来探讨这个主题。今天的节目，为了父亲节，我们为您带来关于孩子试图照顾和保护他们的父亲，以及父亲试图保护和照顾他们的孩子的故事。

00:37:51.650 --> 00:38:21.350
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, Age of Enchantment. Lawrence Weschler is an author and journalist. He used to write a lot for The New Yorker magazine. This story is about his attempt to protect his daughter, Sarah, from disappointment and to thrill her. And how, with the best of intentions, all of this went awry and led to an odd breach of trust between father and child. He and Sarah went into a studio in New York City to tell us this story. At the time, Sarah was 11 years old. The story begins simply enough.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，“魔幻时代”。劳伦斯·韦斯勒是一位作家和记者。他过去常为《纽约客》杂志撰稿。这个故事是关于他试图保护他的女儿莎拉免受失望并让她感到兴奋。以及这一切如何在最好的初衷下，却适得其反，导致了父女之间奇怪的信任破坏。他和莎拉走进纽约市的一间录音室来告诉我们这个故事。当时，莎拉11岁。故事开始得很简单。

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:38.990
<v Lawrence Weschler>She would get into very active conversations with the characters in the books while we were reading. So, for example, when we were reading Little House on the Prairie there would be these moments where she would interrupt my reading and say, "Wait a second. I want to talk to the Indian." And we'd have to go look for a picture of the Indian.
<v Lawrence Weschler>我们在读书时，她会与书中的角色进行非常活跃的对话。例如，当我们在读《大草原上的小木屋》时，会出现一些时刻，她会打断我的阅读说：“等一下。我想跟印第安人说话。”我们就得去寻找印第安人的照片。

00:38:38.990 --> 00:39:00.170
<v Lawrence Weschler>And she'd say to the Indian, "Now look, Indian, in a few pages you're going to meet Laura, but you've got to understand. I know she's taking your land, but it's not her fault. She's just a kid. Now, let me talk to Laura." And we'd go back and we'd talk to Laura. And in these things I would take on the role of the Indian, and I'd say things like, "Who's that talking," and so forth. And we would have these incredibly elaborate conversations. Do you remember that, Sarah?
<v Lawrence Weschler>她会对印第安人说，“现在听着，印第安人，再过几页你就会遇见劳拉，但你得明白。我知道她在抢占你的土地，但这不是她的错。她只是个孩子。现在，让我跟劳拉说话。”然后我们回去和劳拉说话。在这些事情中，我会扮演印第安人的角色，我会说像“是谁在说话”之类的话。我们会进行极其复杂的对话。你记得吗，莎拉？

