00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:08.320
<v Sarah Weschler>Things like that.
<v Sarah Weschler>总之，她会把这些东西留在那。她会非常失望，失望到近乎绝望，因为东西没被拿走。她会跟我进行长谈。她说：“为什么他们不拿走？他们不知道我在给他们吗？”我会试着解释，也许，他们很害怕、紧张，或者别的什么，借东西的人就是那样。

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:28.740
<v Lawrence Weschler>Anyway, so she would leave these things there. And she would be so disappointed, and disappointment verging on desolation, that they weren't picked up. And she'd have long conversations with me. She said, "Why aren't they picking them up? Don't they know that I'm giving it to them?" And I would try to explain that, maybe, they were scared, or nervous, or something, that's how borrowers are.
<v Lawrence Weschler>但她太难过了。这种情况持续了下去。我以为这会结束，但在某个时候，这种情况持续了一周。我不知道我为什么要这么做，因为它引发了一连串后果。但有一天晚上，我捡起那些东西放进了口袋。第二天早上，她跑上楼说：“爸爸！你不会相信的！真的有借东西的人！他们拿走了东西！他们拿走了东西！”她欣喜若狂。我以为那是结束了，但并没有。

00:42:28.740 --> 00:43:04.580
<v Lawrence Weschler>But she was so sad. And this went on. I figured this would end but, at some point, this went on for a week. And I don't know why I did it because it began a cascade of consequences. But one night I picked up the stuff and put it in my pocket. And, the next morning, she came bounding up the stairs saying, "Daddy! You won't believe it! There are borrowers just like I said! They took the stuff! They took the stuff!" And she was transported with delight. And I figured that would be the end of it, but it wasn't.
