00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:56.900
<v Ira Glass>And, Ren, during this time, were you frightened about where this was all going to lead, that at some point you would get found out?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，这变得很奇怪，实际上，有点让人神经紧张。我会做一些事情。我一直以为莎拉会长大而不再相信这些，或者莎拉会联想到这就像我们过去我读印第安人或劳拉故事时所做的那样。我一直以为她会喜欢那样，但她越来越投入其中。它变得越来越复杂。而且投入越深，我越能看出莎拉在其中投入了多少。

00:45:56.900 --> 00:46:32.120
<v Lawrence Weschler>Well, it was getting strange and, actually, kind of nerve-wracking. And I would do things. I kept on figuring that Sarah was going to grow out of this or that Sarah would make the association that this was kind of like what we used to do when I would read about the Indian or about Laura. I kept on thinking that she would just enjoy that, but she was getting more and more into it. And it was becoming more and more involved. And the more involved, the more I could see how invested Sarah was in it.
<v Lawrence Weschler>我的意思是，在那段时间里，这确实是她生活中最主要的事情。当她开始告诉朋友这件事时，故事必须变得越来越复杂，以包含所有渗入细节的零碎部分。我不太知道它会去向何方。我会做一些事情。我会把借东西的人送去度假。

00:46:32.120 --> 00:46:54.690
<v Lawrence Weschler>I mean, it really was the main thing going on in her life during that season. And, as she began telling friends about it and so forth, the stories had to get more and more elaborate to include all the stray bits of details that were seeping into things. And I didn't quite know where it was going to go. I would do things. I would send the borrowers on vacations.
<v Lawrence Weschler>你把他们送去度假？

00:46:54.690 --> 00:46:56.120
<v Ira Glass>You would send them on vacations?
<v Ira Glass>我只会让他们突然消失一会儿。他们会消失一段时间。我希望到结束的时候，莎拉已经忘了。如果我说他们会离开三周，三周后在那一天，莎拉会给他们留一张纸条。

00:46:56.120 --> 00:47:11.550
<v Lawrence Weschler>I'd just have them suddenly disappear for a while. And they'd be gone for a while. And I would hope that, by the time it was over, Sarah would have forgotten. If I had said they'd be gone three weeks, there weeks later on that day there'd be a note for them from Sarah.
<v Lawrence Weschler>莎拉，你当时怀疑了吗？

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:14.060
<v Ira Glass>And, Sarah, did you suspect at that point?
<v Ira Glass>没有。没有。就在我怀疑的那一刻，我几乎确定那一直是他。我只是跑上去问他是不是他。

00:47:14.060 --> 00:47:26.750
<v Sarah Weschler>No. No. The second I suspected it, I was almost sure that it had been him. And I just went up and asked him if it was him.
<v Sarah Weschler>为什么你会开始怀疑？你记得发生了什么让你怀疑吗？

00:47:26.750 --> 00:47:31.570
<v Ira Glass>Why is it that you started to suspect? Do you remember what happened that made you suspect?
<v Ira Glass>我想是因为事实上——其实不是他的笔迹。而是我告诉爸爸，例如，我在地下室，我把手指伸进这个洞里，我感觉到了一些柔软或什么的。那可能只是卡在那里的一块旧布。但我感觉到了，我告诉了爸爸。它从我手指滑走了。我告诉了爸爸这件事。

00:47:31.570 --> 00:47:53.790
<v Sarah Weschler>I think it was sort of the fact-- not his handwriting, actually. It was that I would tell my dad, for instance, that I was in the basement and I stuck my finger into this hole, and I felt something sort of silky or something. It was probably just something, an old piece of cloth that was stuck there. But I felt it, and I told my dad. And it sort of slipped away from my finger. And told my dad about it.
<v Sarah Weschler>然后，在下一封信里，安娜贝尔·李就会说，“哦，是你触碰我时我穿着丝绸连衣裙吗？”所以我开始想，我会告诉爸爸一些事情，有时，我会夸大一点。当我年轻的时候，我夸大了一些事情。我让事情比它们本来可能的样子更令人兴奋了一点。

00:47:53.790 --> 00:48:14.550
<v Sarah Weschler>And then, in the next letter, Annabel Lee'd be saying, "Oh, was that you who touched me when I was wearing my silk dress? And so I started to think I tell my dad things that, sometimes, I'd exaggerate a little bit. When I was younger, I exaggerated some things. I made things a little bit more exciting than they might have really been.
<v Sarah Weschler>然后我读了那封信，里面有夸大的部分。所以我开始想，那并没有真正发生。我只是把它加到我的故事里了。所以我——

00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:27.790
<v Sarah Weschler>And then I read the letter, and it had that exaggerated part in it. And so I started to go, that didn't really happen. I was just adding that to my story. And so I got--
<v Sarah Weschler>那让你产生了怀疑？

00:48:27.790 --> 00:48:28.410
<v Ira Glass>That made you suspicious?
<v Ira Glass>是的。我问了爸爸，然后——

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:30.490
<v Sarah Weschler>Yeah. And I asked my dad, and--
<v Sarah Weschler>所以发生的事情是我们搬到了新房子。到那时我们已经住了几个月了。我在地下室搬动一些盒子。莎拉下来了。

00:48:30.490 --> 00:48:42.290
<v Lawrence Weschler>So what happened there was we had moved to the new house. We had been there for a couple of months at that point. And I was down in the basement moving some boxes around. And Sarah came down there.
<v Lawrence Weschler>多少岁？

00:48:42.290 --> 00:48:44.160
<v Ira Glass>And, how old?
<v Ira Glass>她现在八岁。是的。

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:45.910
<v Lawrence Weschler>She's now eight. Yeah.
<v Lawrence Weschler>好的。在这个故事里，是的。

00:48:45.910 --> 00:48:47.310
<v Ira Glass>OK. In this story, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>在故事的这一点上，是的。她开始了。她的嘴唇在颤抖。她的下唇在颤抖。她很坚定地看着我，像她非常有能力做到的那样。她说：“爸爸，我现在要问你一个问题，你必须说实话，因为爸爸对女儿撒谎是一种罪过。”我的心沉了下去。

00:48:47.310 --> 00:49:11.390
<v Lawrence Weschler>At this point in the story, yeah. And she began. Her lips were trembling. Her lower lip was trembling. And she looked at me very firmly as she is quite capable of doing. And she said, "Daddy, I'm going to ask you a question now, and you have to tell the truth because it's a sin for daddies to lie to their daughters." And my heart just sank.
<v Lawrence Weschler>她说，“爸爸，是你一直在写安娜贝尔·李的字条吗？”我看着她。她看着我。有五六秒钟的沉默。然后我说，“你知道，这有点复杂。我们能谈谈——”她说，“爸爸，这不复杂。很简单。是你吗？”我说，“好吧，我们能晚点谈吗？”她说，“不。告诉我。到底是不是你？”我深吸了一口气说，“是的，是我。”然后她爆发了——

00:49:11.390 --> 00:49:47.910
<v Lawrence Weschler>And she said, "Daddy, are you the one who's been writing Annabel Lee's notes?" And I looked at her. And she looked at me. And there was silence for five or six seconds. And then I said, "You know, it's kind of complicated. Can we talk--" and she said, "Daddy, it's not complicated. It's simple. Are you the one?" And I said, "Well, can we talk about it later?" She said, "No. Just tell me. Are you the one or not?" And I took a big breath and I said, "Yes, it is me." And she broke into--
<v Lawrence Weschler>我在哭。

00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:48.240
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>I was crying.
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>噢，天哪。她在啜泣。

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:49.170
<v Lawrence Weschler>Oh, god. She was sobbing.
<v Lawrence Weschler>我太难过了。

00:49:49.170 --> 00:49:50.080
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>I was so sad.
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>她开始啜泣。这绝对是我为人父母到那时为止发生的最痛苦的事情。我的意思是，我完全搞砸了。我只感到彻底的灾难。我在哭。她在哭。你知道，我们俩都有点互相紧抓着，抱着对方。我们真的陷在那个陷阱里了。我们在那个洞里。我们陷入了大麻烦。

00:49:50.080 --> 00:50:13.710
<v Lawrence Weschler>She started sobbing. It was, easily, the most wrenching thing that had happened in my parenthood up to that point. I mean, I had totally blown it. I just felt total disaster. And I was crying. And she was crying. And, you know, we were both kind of clutching each other and holding each other. And we were really in a trap there. We were down the hole at that point. We were in big trouble.
<v Lawrence Weschler>突然，莎拉脸上出现了一种平静。就像早晨升起的太阳。她的前额不再紧锁。它变得平滑了。她只是看着我说，“爸爸，你没意识到吗？你毁了一切，因为借东西的人是真的。而且你在他们能够拿到信之前就把信拿走了。”她解决了所有的问题，因为，除其他事项外，那正是她能够告诉朋友的事情。她们都可以对她有一个多么疯狂的父亲而咯咯大笑。这太惊人了。她找到了一种让我们摆脱这场我本应为我们打造的灾难的方法。

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:54.070
<v Lawrence Weschler>And, suddenly, this kind of calm came over Sarah's face. It was kind of like the sun rising in the morning. And her forehead stopped being furrowed. It became smooth. And she just looked at me, and she said, "Daddy, don't you realize? You've ruined everything because there are borrowers. And you were taking the letters before they were able to get them." And she had solved everything there because, among other things, that  was what she was going to be able to tell her friends. And they could all chortle about what kind of a crazy father she had. And it was amazing. She found a way of getting us out of this disaster that, I suppose, I had fashioned for us.
<v Lawrence Weschler>因为我记得你说过你应该把它留在那里。也许他们真的会回信。你不该那样做，因为也许他们最后真的会在某个时候给我回信。

00:50:54.070 --> 00:51:04.890
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>Because I remember saying that you should have left it there. Maybe they would have really written back. You shouldn't have done it because maybe they would have actually written back to me, finally, at some point.
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>正如我之前所说的，我仍然相信他们。我知道这对一些可能会听这个的孩子来说可能听起来很幼稚，但我仍然相信他们。当我告诉梅根，我的朋友，当我告诉梅根那是我爸爸时，她不再相信他们了。从那时起，只要我提到它，她就会嘲笑我，告诉我，“哦，莎拉，别这么幼稚。”因为她比我大一岁，所以当时，她仍然认为自己比我优秀，尽管我们是最好的朋友。她说，“哦，莎拉，别这么幼稚。这不是真的。这不是真的。你可以停止想象它了。别再跟我说了，因为这不是真的。”

00:51:04.890 --> 00:51:51.730
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>Like I said earlier, I still believe in them. And I know that may sound really baby-ish to some kids who might listen to this, but I still believe in them. And when I told Megan, my friend, when I told Megan that it had been my dad, she stopped believing in them. And she'd just, whenever I'd talk about it from then on, she'd laugh at me and tell me, "Oh, Sarah, stop being a baby." Because she's a year older than me, so she, at that time, she still considered herself really superior to me even though we were best friends. And she said, "Oh, Sarah, stop being a baby. It's not true. It's just not true." And I said, "But I've seen them." And she said, "No, you haven't. You just imagined it. And it's not true. And you can just stop imagining it. And stop telling me about it because it's not true."
<v Sasha Meyerowitz>当你现在看着那些信时，你感觉如何？

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:55.390
<v Lawrence Weschler>How do you feel about it now when you look at those letters?
<v Lawrence Weschler>我不知道。有时候，当我读它们时，我仍然会想，“我不知道为什么这发生在她身上？我不知道为什么那个发生在她身上？我不知道她为什么会那样说？”即使我知道是我爸爸写的，我有时仍然认为在外面某个地方有一个安娜贝尔·李。

00:51:55.390 --> 00:52:14.300
<v Sarah Weschler>I don't know. Sometimes, when I read them, I still can think, "I wonder why this happened to her? I wonder why that happened to her? I wonder why she would say that?" Even though I'd know that it was my dad writing to me, I still, sometimes, think of there being an Annabel Lee somewhere out there.
<v Sarah Weschler>昨晚我们拿出盒子看那些信的时候，看那些信给你带来了快乐吗？

00:52:14.300 --> 00:52:20.000
<v Lawrence Weschler>When we pulled out the box last night, of letters, did it bring you pleasure to look at those letters?
<v Lawrence Weschler>嗯，其实，我经常看它。

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:21.550
<v Sarah Weschler>Well, actually, I look at it a lot.
<v Sarah Weschler>你经常看？

00:52:21.550 --> 00:52:22.640
<v Lawrence Weschler>You do?
<v Lawrence Weschler>是的。

00:52:22.640 --> 00:52:23.080
<v Sarah Weschler>Yeah.
<v Sarah Weschler>你经常看它？

00:52:23.080 --> 00:52:23.950
<v Ira Glass>You look at it a lot?
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:52:23.950 --> 00:52:24.740
<v Sarah Weschler>Yeah.
<v Sarah Weschler>你看着它时想什么？

00:52:24.740 --> 00:52:26.680
<v Ira Glass>And what do you think when you look at it?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，现在回想起来，他那样做还是挺好的。因为我记得，当它发生时，在我意识到是他之后，我问过他，“嗯，我们还能互相写信吗？”但我们之后其实再也没有互相写过信。

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:46.780
<v Sarah Weschler>Well, now, looking back, it was sort of nice of him to do that. Because I remember, when it was happening and after I had figured out that it was him, I had asked him, "Well, can we still write to each other?" And we never really, actually, wrote to each other after that.
<v Sarah Weschler>但我只是想，过了一段时间，这是一件好事。而且，即使，也许没有借东西的人给我写信，也许，让我爸爸编造这一整家人，也许，一样特别。或者也许几乎和真的给借东西的人写信一样特别。

00:52:46.780 --> 00:53:09.980
<v Sarah Weschler>But I just thought, after a while, that it was a nice thing. And that, even though, maybe there was no borrower writing to me, there was, maybe, having my dad make up this whole family was, maybe, just as special. Or maybe almost as special as having actually been writing to a borrower.
<v Sarah Weschler>莎拉，我可以问你，你认为这个故事的教训是什么吗？也就是说，如果父母听你讲这个故事，你和你爸爸在广播里讲这个故事，如果另一个父母陷入这种境地，你给他们的建议是什么？他们应该随波逐流吗？他们应该写信，他们应该捡起东西吗？

00:53:09.980 --> 00:53:29.060
<v Ira Glass>Sarah, can I ask you, what do you think the lesson of this story is? That is, if parents hear you tell this story, you and your dad tell this story on the radio, and, if another parent gets into this kind of situation, what's your advice for them? Should they go along with it? Should they write letters, and should they pick up stuff?
<v Ira Glass>我不知道，因为那种体验对我来说真的很有趣。但是，当我发现是我爸爸写的，真的很让人心烦。所以我只是——我不知道。我想我不会。如果我是父母，我有那种情况，我不会捡起来。

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:45.140
<v Sarah Weschler>I don't know, because it was really fun for me to have this kind of experience. But, when I found out that it was my dad writing, it was really upsetting. And so I just-- I don't know. I think that I wouldn't. If I were a parent and I had that kind of thing, I would not pick it up.
<v Sarah Weschler>你不会？

00:53:45.140 --> 00:53:45.980
<v Ira Glass>You would not?
<v Ira Glass>不。我会鼓励我的孩子继续给借东西的人写信，并试着让他们回信，但我不会捡起来。

00:53:45.980 --> 00:53:54.490
<v Sarah Weschler>No. I would keep encouraging my kid and my child to keep on writing to the borrowers and trying to get them to write back, but I wouldn't pick it up.
<v Sarah Weschler>如果他们每天早上都那么难过地来到你面前，像你当时难过地来到我面前一样，只是恳求，“我希望。我希望。我希望他们会来？”你能想象自己会捡起来吗？

00:53:54.490 --> 00:54:04.670
<v Lawrence Weschler>What if they keep coming to you so sad every morning, the way you were sad coming to me, and just pleading, "I wish. I wish. I wish they would come?" Can you imagine ever picking it up?
<v Lawrence Weschler>不能。

00:54:04.670 --> 00:54:05.340
<v Sarah Weschler>No.
<v Sarah Weschler>真的吗？

00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:05.800
<v Lawrence Weschler>Really?
<v Lawrence Weschler>我觉得这样诱导别人是不公平的。

00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:11.290
<v Sarah Weschler>I don't think it's fair to lead someone on like that.
<v Sarah Weschler>伦，就你而言，这个故事的教训是什么？如果你能重来，如果你再次陷入这种情况，或者如果你能带着后见之明回去，你会怎么做？你会留下那些信吗？

00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:25.790
<v Ira Glass>Ren, as far as you're concerned, what's the lesson of this story? If you had this to do again, if you would get into this situation again or if you could go back with the benefit of hindsight, what would you do? Would you have left the letters?
<v Ira Glass>我的意思是，我想说，如果能重活一次，我不会这样做。但我不敢肯定，因为它开始得太自然了。最终，顺便说一句，我必须说的是，我生命中某个阶段最感人、最亲密、最不可思议的时刻，就是我们在地下室互相紧抱着，我们两个都在哭，但莎拉没有跑开，莎拉救了我们。那以一种非常美妙的方式巩固了我们的关系。

00:54:25.790 --> 00:55:10.120
<v Lawrence Weschler>I mean, I'd like to say that, had I to live it over again, I wouldn't do it this way. But I'm not sure because it started so naturally. And, in the end, by the way, what I'd have to say is probably the most poignant, closest, amazing moment I've had, you know, the moment I'll remember of a particular phase of my life, is the holding onto each other in the basement, both of us crying, but Sarah not running away and Sarah saving us. And that kind of cemented our relationship in a really wonderful way.
<v Lawrence Weschler>并不是对每个人来说结果都会是那样。

00:55:10.120 --> 00:55:12.710
<v Sarah Weschler>It might not end that way for everybody.
<v Sarah Weschler>是的，并不是对每个人来说结果都会是那样。那是真的。我继续琢磨它。它对我来说还没解决，就像我的回答所表明的那样，我想。

00:55:12.710 --> 00:55:22.930
<v Lawrence Weschler>Yeah, it might not end that way for everybody. That's true. I continue to puzzle about it. And it is unresolved for me, as my answer is indicating, I suppose.
<v Lawrence Weschler>这很有趣，我觉得你对这个故事教训的看法是莎拉救了你们两个。作为父母，你让自己陷入了一个你真的不知道该怎么办的时刻，最后是她说的话让一切都变好了。

00:55:22.930 --> 00:55:38.950
<v Ira Glass>It's interesting, to me that the way that you view the lesson of this story is that Sarah saved the two of you. That, as a parent, you got yourself into a moment where you literally didn't know what to do, and that she finally said the thing that made everything OK.
<v Ira Glass>我绝对有这种感觉。你认为它影响了我们的关系吗？你不像以前那样信任我了吗？

00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:47.070
<v Lawrence Weschler>I absolutely feel that. Has it affected our relationship, do you think? Do you not trust me in the way you used to trust me?
<v Lawrence Weschler>不。不。不，我仍然信任你。

00:55:47.070 --> 00:55:49.540
<v Sarah Weschler>No. No. No, I still trust you.
<v Sarah Weschler>莎拉，你认为这是你与父亲最亲密的时刻之一吗？

00:55:49.540 --> 00:55:53.340
<v Ira Glass>Sarah, do you view this as one of the moments when you were closest to your dad?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我和爸爸很亲密，所以我不知道。这就像，是的，我想是吧。但这不像是我通常那样的亲密，因为我可能一直都和爸爸很亲密。但，是的，这是我最亲密的时刻之一，我想。是的。

00:55:53.340 --> 00:56:11.270
<v Sarah Weschler>Well, I'm very close my dad, so I don't know. It's like, yeah, I guess so. But it's not like much closer than I am usually because I'm probably close to my dad all the time. But, yeah, it is one of the times that I was closest, I guess. Yeah.
<v Sarah Weschler>所以，我的心都在嗓子眼了。

00:56:11.270 --> 00:56:19.040
<v Lawrence Weschler>So, my heart is in my throat.
<v Lawrence Weschler>劳伦斯·韦斯勒，他现在是芝加哥人文艺术节的艺术总监。他也是许多书的作者，包括他最新的书，《一切上升的东西》，《融合之书》。

00:56:19.040 --> 00:56:28.930
<v Ira Glass>Lawrence Weschler, he's now the artistic director of the Chicago Humanities Festival. And he's the author of many books, including his latest, Everything That Rises , A Book of Convergences.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉现在19岁。过去一年，她在坦桑尼亚的一个偏远村庄教幼儿园。秋天她就要上大学了。当我们在八年前录制这次采访时，她说即使在我们录制采访的时候她仍然相信借东西的人。事实上，她说她会听这个故事，但不是在屋里。她要在车里听。

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:48.160
<v Ira Glass>Sarah is now 19. This past year, she's been teaching kindergarten in a remote village in Tanzania. She goes off to college in the fall. Back when we recorded this interview, eight years ago, she said that even at the time that we were recording the interview she still believed in the borrowers. In fact, she said she was going to listen to the story, but not in the house. She was going to listen in the car.
<v Ira Glass>我不想我们在家里听它，因为如果屋子里还有借东西的人，我不想让他们听到这个，然后认为他们不能信任我。因为，现在，我正在讲他们的整个故事，所以我感觉我好像是在背叛他们。我只是想确保他们知道这一点，你知道吗？如果我真的遇见一个，我不会告诉任何人。我永远不会告诉世界上任何一个人。

00:56:48.160 --> 00:57:32.300
<v Sarah Weschler>I don't want us to listen to it at the house because, if there are still borrowers in our house, I don't want them to hear that and think that they can't trust me. Because, right now, I'm telling their whole story, so I feel like I'm sort of betraying them. And I just wanted to make sure that they knew that, you know? If I actually did meet one, I wouldn't tell anyone. I would never tell a single person in the world.
<v Sarah Weschler>我们的节目今天由朱莉·斯奈德和我自己制作，还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、南希·厄普代克和豪尔赫·贾斯特。本节目的特约编辑有保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林和顾问萨拉·沃维尔。托德·巴克曼、西尔维娅·莱姆斯、萨蒂瓦·詹纳里和塞斯·林德提供了制作协助。

